  "segments": [
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "After Fedya and Senya mock Max in the kitchen as a lowly waiter, Max decides to get revenge on Chef Barinov by recommending Katya's molecular menu to Nastya's hungry father.",
      "gap": 0.8
    },
    {
      "v": "katya",
      "t": "Мы ещё посмотрим!",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "guest",
      "t": "Я проголодался.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "guest",
      "t": "Господа, давайте сделаем обеденный перерыв.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "guest",
      "t": "Потом подпишем.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "guest",
      "t": "Максим, что тут у вас вкусного?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Знаете, у нас появилось новое меню.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Молекулярная кухня. Пожалуйста.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "guest",
      "t": "Химия?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Нет, всё натуральное.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "guest",
      "t": "Как в вашей колбасе? Тогда неси.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Спагетти и утка, четыре порции!",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "katya",
      "t": "Принято!",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Now let's go back through their conversation line by line and unpack the Russian phrasing.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "katya",
      "t": "Мы ещё посмотрим!",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'We will see about that yet!' The meaning hinges on 'we' working together with 'we shall see' in spoken Russian.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "guest",
      "t": "Я проголодался.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I have gotten hungry.' the guest anchors the line on 'I', while 'got hungry' completes the thought.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "guest",
      "t": "Господа, давайте сделаем обеденный перерыв.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Gentlemen, let's take a lunch break.' In Russian, the idea of 'gentlemen' flows naturally into 'break' without extra filler words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "guest",
      "t": "Потом подпишем.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'We will sign afterward.' Spoken Russian keeps this brisk, leaning on 'then' and finishing on 'will sign'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "guest",
      "t": "Максим, что тут у вас вкусного?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Maxim, what do you have that is delicious here?' Notice how Russian expresses 'what' and 'tasty' with crisp inflection rather than helper words. Max answers right away.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Знаете, у нас появилось новое меню.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'You know, we have a new menu.' Max anchors the line on 'know', while 'menu' completes the thought.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Молекулярная кухня. Пожалуйста.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Molecular cuisine. Here you go.' Max brings out the contrast between 'molecular' and 'cuisine' in a few short words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "guest",
      "t": "Химия?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Chemicals?' Notice how Russian expresses 'chemistry' and 'chemistry' with crisp inflection rather than helper words. Max answers right away.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Нет, всё натуральное.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'No, everything is natural.' The grammar packs 'no' and 'natural' into a tight, idiomatic kitchen exchange.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "guest",
      "t": "Как в вашей колбасе? Тогда неси.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Like in your sausage? Then bring it.' The meaning hinges on 'how' working together with 'bring' in spoken Russian.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Спагетти и утка, четыре порции!",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Spaghetti and duck, four portions!' In Russian, the idea of 'spaghetti' flows naturally into 'portions' without extra filler words. Katya answers right away.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "katya",
      "t": "Принято!",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Order received!' Spoken Russian keeps this brisk, leaning on 'accepted' and finishing on 'accepted'. Alright, let's hear the entire conversation one more time from start to finish.",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
